WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.790
Easter eggs are usually fun: sidebar references,
visual gags, or inside jokes that directors

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:08.730
put in movies as a reward for eagle-eyed viewers.

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:14.300
But some filmmakers use them as opportunities
to startle—or outright frighten—audiences.

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:18.690
These movie Easter eggs are so creepy, we
almost wish we'd never found them at all.

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:19.870
Trophy room

00:00:19.870 --> 00:00:23.500
You don't have to watch the Alien movies to
know Xenomorphs are bad news.

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:27.040
If something out there is tough enough to
actually exterminate one of these things,

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:28.780
they've gotta be super hardcore.

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:30.750
And we're not just talking about Ripley.

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:33.480
"Not bad for a human."

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:40.679
So if Predator 2 really wanted to hammer home
how powerful those Predators really are are,

00:00:40.679 --> 00:00:43.609
having them take down a Xenomorph would be
a good way to do it.

00:00:43.609 --> 00:00:44.769
Well, mission accomplished.

00:00:44.769 --> 00:00:49.710
Just before the film's climactic battle against
the Predators, Danny Glover's character, Lt.

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:53.569
Mike Harrington, comes across a case filled
with various skulls.

00:00:53.569 --> 00:00:58.010
For about a second, the camera shows what
is clearly a Xenomorph skull, which is as

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:00.940
much a proof as any that Predators are not
to be messed with.

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:05.080
If they can kill some of the most vicious
aliens in the galaxy, steer clear.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:10.320
This brief crossover predated the AvP franchise
by over a decade, though the jury's still

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:12.750
out on whether that wait was worth it.

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:15.409
Ash vs. Freddy

00:01:15.409 --> 00:01:19.630
Directors Sam Raimi and Wes Craven really
like to reference one another, as evidenced

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:21.930
by the several macabre Easter eggs between
them.

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:26.770
The first came by way of A Nightmare on Elm
Street, when Nancy is desperate to stay awake...

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:28.130
so she watches Evil Dead.

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:32.420
It barely works, and she soon shuts it off
in favor of other methods of staying awake.

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:37.000
In Evil Dead 2, Raimi returned the favor by
including several brief shots of Freddy Krueger's

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:40.770
glove hanging over the door in the shed where
Ash gets his chainsaw hand.

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:45.249
Apparently, this reference was so nice he
had to do it twice—in the series Ash vs.

00:01:45.249 --> 00:01:47.969
Evil Dead, the glove appeared again.

00:01:47.969 --> 00:01:49.009
Groovy.

00:01:49.009 --> 00:01:50.200
Mid-world Pennywise

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:54.299
Stephen King loves to pepper his books with
references to his other works.

00:01:54.299 --> 00:01:58.130
He's like Pixar, but you know, if every horrifying
character was Sid.

00:01:58.130 --> 00:02:01.080
"Where are your rebel friends now?

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:02.080
Hahaha."

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:07.590
The 2017 adaptation of King's The Dark Tower
series honors this rich tradition with one

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:09.580
of the creepiest Easter eggs of all.

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:13.120
As Jake enters Mid-World in the film, he walks
through an abandoned carnival.

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:17.580
At one point, he comes across a statue of
a severed hand holding three balloons.

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:20.500
And what creepy Stephen King character loves
his balloons?

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:25.030
Yep, that would be Pennywise, the scariest
clown this side of the sewer system.

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:29.490
And just in case you weren't sure, above Jake
stands a large, crumbling sign that clearly

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:30.700
says PENNYWISE.

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:34.910
In addition to being pretty freaky, this egg
actually makes narrative sense.

00:02:34.910 --> 00:02:37.450
Pennywise can be anything and and go anywhere.

00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:40.300
So why wouldn't he chill in Mid-World, looking
for a meal or two?

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:43.280
After all, they float over there too.

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:44.740
Overlooked design

00:02:44.740 --> 00:02:48.740
For a children's cartoon, Toy Story is pretty
creepy to start with.

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:53.050
The idea that toys are alive and watching
their little owners at all times is a little

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:56.820
unsettling—and the filmmakers made things
even weirder by including references to one

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:59.350
of the creepiest films ever: The Shining.

00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:03.780
In the first Toy Story, take a look at the
carpet in the upstairs hallway of Sid's house.

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:07.910
If the pattern seems familiar, it's because
it first appeared in the Overlook Hotel in

00:03:07.910 --> 00:03:10.420
Stanley Kubrick's adaptation of the The Shining.

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:14.680
And even freakier, the carpet is prominently
featured in a scene when Danny plays with

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:15.680
his toys.

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:20.491
So not only is Sid a psychopathic toy torturer
who probably belongs on a FBI watchlist, he

00:03:20.491 --> 00:03:23.410
also lives in the house-sized version of the
Overlook.

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:27.730
Not content with just making creepy references
in one movie, Pixar decided to load up Toy

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:29.850
Story 3 with even more.

00:03:29.850 --> 00:03:34.490
In the control room of the daycare center,
the intercom is an exact copy of the one in

00:03:34.490 --> 00:03:38.980
the Overlook manager's office, and the box
of tissues is printed with that carpet design

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:39.980
again.

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:44.630
The number 237, the number of the haunted
hotel room in The Shining, also shows up several

00:03:44.630 --> 00:03:45.630
times.

00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:48.580
"Who's Velocistar237?"

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:49.160
"Ooh!

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:50.680
That's just a dinosaur toy down the street.

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:52.340
That's nothing.

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:53.660
Let me just take care of that."

00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:58.700
The number appears again on a security camera
labeled OVERLOOK R237 and on the license plate

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:00.140
of a garbage truck.

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:04.380
Either the Pixar crew just wants to creep
out kids subconsciously, or they just really

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:05.970
like horror movies.

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:07.840
An unfortunate cameo

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:11.440
Gyro Captain is one of the weirder characters
in Mad Max: The Road Warrior.

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:16.000
The unhinged gyrocopter pilot became the leader
of the oil refinery tribe and led them north

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:18.800
at the end of the movie, never to be heard
from again.

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:20.770
At least until Fury Road.

00:04:20.770 --> 00:04:25.300
Nux's car has a skull on one of its pikes—and
not just any skull, but the skull of a pilot

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:29.080
with a flight helmet that looks creepily similar
to the Gyro Captain's.

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:33.030
The design is a little different, but how
many other characters in the Mad Max franchise

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:34.380
wear flight helmets?

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:39.170
If that really is the Gyro Captain's head,
it makes Road Warrior's ending more bittersweet,

00:04:39.170 --> 00:04:43.780
because it suggests that the Warboys caught
up with the oil refinery tribe, executed their

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:45.970
leaders, and took them into the Citadel.

00:04:45.970 --> 00:04:48.840
Whatever happened to them, it can't be pretty.

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:50.450
Puppet show

00:04:50.450 --> 00:04:53.890
Creepy puppets are spine-tinglingly scary
when they're done right.

00:04:53.890 --> 00:04:57.880
Although the Saw franchise has had its ups
and downs over the years, Billy the Puppet

00:04:57.880 --> 00:05:02.990
remains consistently creepy—and he isn't
content to spook us in just one franchise.

00:05:02.990 --> 00:05:04.500
He keeps showing up in others.

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:08.890
Take, for example, Dead Silence, a movie about
evil, life-ending dolls.

00:05:08.890 --> 00:05:13.420
During a scene in which the main characters
explore an abandoned theater, there's a brief

00:05:13.420 --> 00:05:15.900
shot showing Billy with the other paranormal
dolls.

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:20.180
The whole place is literally a creepy doll
club where they just all hang out together—which

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:22.910
makes that the creepiest theater in existence.

00:05:22.910 --> 00:05:25.570
But our favorite killer puppet didn't stop
there.

00:05:25.570 --> 00:05:28.230
Insidious is on Billy's list of cameos, too.

00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:32.510
Early in the movie, a rough sketch of Billy
shows up on the blackboard in a classroom.

00:05:32.510 --> 00:05:36.610
From a different angle, you can see another,
less creepy Easter egg on the same board:

00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:39.050
The director's name, James Wan.

00:05:39.050 --> 00:05:43.661
Since Wan directed Dead Silence, Insidious,
and Saw, we guess it makes sense that he'd

00:05:43.661 --> 00:05:47.600
include these Easter eggs, even if it creeps
the audience out by implying that Billy is

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:50.160
following them into other movies.

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:51.230
Doctor Doom

00:05:51.230 --> 00:05:55.950
When Jyn bumped into Dr. Evazan and Ponda
Baba on Jedha, it seemed like a fun little

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:57.270
nod to A New Hope.

00:05:57.270 --> 00:06:02.180
We knew that within a week Dr. Evazan would
be threatening Luke Skywalker in Mos Eisley,

00:06:02.180 --> 00:06:04.139
and Ponda Baba would be short one arm.

00:06:04.139 --> 00:06:07.760
For some reason, though, the production team
couldn't let this little moment stand as a

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:11.910
simple nod to the original film—they had
to create one of the more disturbing Easter

00:06:11.910 --> 00:06:12.910
eggs in Star Wars history.

00:06:12.910 --> 00:06:17.981
In A New Hope, Dr. Evazan tells Luke that
he has a "death sentence" in 12 systems, but

00:06:17.981 --> 00:06:19.639
we never really find out why.

00:06:19.639 --> 00:06:21.600
Rogue One fills in the gaps.

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:25.210
Eagle-eyed viewers might have noticed that
a few background citizens on Jedha had their

00:06:25.210 --> 00:06:26.680
heads cut in half.

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:28.730
These poor people are the "Decraniated."

00:06:28.730 --> 00:06:33.160
A mysterious doctor named Roofoo created them
by destroying their brains and making them

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:34.160
his slaves.

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:35.320
Even more disturbing?

00:06:35.320 --> 00:06:40.230
The official Star Wars website confirmed that
Roofoo is an alias for Dr. Evazan, turning

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:44.030
a fun little Easter egg into a part of a full-blown
horror story.

00:06:44.030 --> 00:06:47.330
Now we're even happier that Obi-Wan dealt
with those creeps before they could mess with

00:06:47.330 --> 00:06:48.590
Luke's brain.

00:06:48.590 --> 00:06:50.250
Norwegian spoiler

00:06:50.250 --> 00:06:55.260
In the opening scene of the 1982 version of
The Thing, American researchers discover a

00:06:55.260 --> 00:06:57.770
Norwegian crew trying to dispatch a dog.

00:06:57.770 --> 00:07:01.770
One terrified member of the party is screaming
something at the Americans, but there aren't

00:07:01.770 --> 00:07:06.720
any subtitles, so for all English-speaking
audiences can tell, he's just yelling gibberish

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:07.940
brought on by madness.

00:07:14.900 --> 00:07:19.470
If you speak Norwegian, however, the movie
immediately becomes even more terrifying.

00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:21.900
What he's actually saying is: "Get the hell
away!

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:22.900
It's not a dog!

00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:23.900
It's a thing!

00:07:23.900 --> 00:07:24.900
It's imitating a dog!

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:25.900
It's not real!

00:07:25.900 --> 00:07:26.900
Get away, idiots!"

00:07:26.900 --> 00:07:30.370
But since none of the American characters
are, you know, fluent in Norwegian, they don't

00:07:30.370 --> 00:07:32.710
understand what the crazy old man is saying.

00:07:32.710 --> 00:07:34.139
And we know what happens next.

00:07:34.140 --> 00:07:35.540
A whooole lot of this:

00:07:40.820 --> 00:07:44.810
It's a creepy little Easter egg that totally
changes the story, turning the whole thing

00:07:44.810 --> 00:07:46.110
into an ironic tragedy.

00:07:46.110 --> 00:07:50.540
We also feel terrible for that researcher
who tried to help them, only to get shot for

00:07:50.540 --> 00:07:51.540
his troubles.

00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:55.590
If only the researchers had brushed up on
their languages, they might have survived.

00:07:55.590 --> 00:07:57.250
X marks the spot

00:07:57.250 --> 00:08:01.449
From the start of The Departed, it's obvious
that most of these characters won't make it

00:08:01.449 --> 00:08:02.890
out of the movie alive.

00:08:02.890 --> 00:08:07.290
But what we didn't expect was for director
Martin Scorsese to physically mark every character

00:08:07.290 --> 00:08:08.290
who wouldn't.

00:08:08.290 --> 00:08:12.430
Yup, every character who gets whacked appears
with an X nearby at some point.

00:08:12.430 --> 00:08:14.880
Some are really obvious, others not so much.

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:19.200
Frank Costello is marked early in the movie,
with the crossbeams of his tire garage forming

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:24.050
an X. Matt Damon's character has Xs around
him all throughout the movie even though doesn't

00:08:24.050 --> 00:08:25.800
bite the bullet until the final act.

00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:30.300
But, of course, there's a big red X on the
carpet right before he finally does meet his

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:31.300
end

00:08:31.300 --> 00:08:35.419
Dedication to this Easter egg reaches almost
comical proportions near the end of the movie

00:08:35.419 --> 00:08:39.669
in the elevator scene, when Costigan stands
right next to a giant tape X.

00:08:39.669 --> 00:08:44.279
Even less subtle are the Xs foretelling Captain
Queenan's end—they show up in the windows

00:08:44.279 --> 00:08:46.639
of the building that he's falling from.

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:50.360
Scorsese's little inside joke turns a cool
gangster movie into a super morbid treasure

00:08:50.360 --> 00:08:51.740
hunt.

00:08:52.180 --> 00:08:53.260
Swan bones

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:58.399
Darren Aronofsky's Black Swan is filled with
background imagery, from morphing faces showing

00:08:58.399 --> 00:09:01.170
true identities to crazy mirror tricks.

00:09:01.170 --> 00:09:05.750
Watch the movie again knowing all of the visual
tricks, and the whole story becomes even more

00:09:05.750 --> 00:09:09.439
unsettling... but the creepiest Easter egg
is found in the ballet director's office.

00:09:09.439 --> 00:09:12.800
Pay attention when Nina goes and asks for
her part.

00:09:12.800 --> 00:09:16.850
Right next to the director's desk is a model
of swan skeleton in front of one of the film's

00:09:16.850 --> 00:09:21.509
many mirrors—an insanely creepy decoration
to just have around in the office, but also

00:09:21.509 --> 00:09:24.829
an unsettling foreshadowing of Nina's eventual
collapse.

00:09:24.829 --> 00:09:29.959
All throughout the movie Aronofsky uses mirrors
to represent Nina's fractured personality,

00:09:29.959 --> 00:09:34.689
so this little detail couldn't be anything
but a reference to Nina's terrible fate.

00:09:34.689 --> 00:09:35.689
Face-off

00:09:35.689 --> 00:09:40.149
As if Psycho didn't have enough terrifying
visuals in it, director Alfred Hitchcock made

00:09:40.149 --> 00:09:43.360
sure to sneak in one more at the end, just
for good measure.

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:48.069
Not content with scaring the audience consciously,
he also wanted to get under their skin.

00:09:48.069 --> 00:09:51.040
Specifically, with the final shot of Norman
Bates.

00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:55.260
"Why, she wouldn't even harm a fly."

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:03.780
That's actually one frame of Norman's face
merged with the corpse of his mother.

00:10:03.790 --> 00:10:08.800
Not only is it terrifying, it shows that Norman's
mother is now in complete control of his persona.

00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:12.959
Hitchcock didn't want any subtlety about how
Norman turns out, and this frame was his way

00:10:12.959 --> 00:10:16.050
of showing one last time how messed up the
character is.

00:10:16.050 --> 00:10:20.480
Not that we really needed the reminder, considering
the guy just spent the whole movie dressing

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:22.300
up like his mom and stabbing people.

00:10:22.300 --> 00:10:23.910
Thanks for watching!

00:10:23.910 --> 00:10:26.619
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:10:26.619 --> 00:10:28.980
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

